# Week 3 Saturday: Load Testing & Max Concurrency Push! 🚀

We successfully implemented **PgBouncer** connection pooling and scaled out the LangGraph background workers to find the absolute maximum concurrency our system can handle! Here is a summary of the findings and how to continue scaling it.

## 📊 Baseline vs. PgBouncer Metrics

We used `k6` to simulate concurrent users chatting with the system.

| Metric | Baseline (20 VUs, 2 workers, NO PgBouncer) | Scaled (20 VUs, 4 workers, WITH PgBouncer) | Max Push (100 VUs, 4 workers) |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Total Iterations** | 61 | 110 (+80% throughput) | 187 |
| **Throughput (req/s)** | 1.45/s | 2.99/s | 3.11/s |
| **Avg Request Duration** | 5.38s | 2.51s (2x faster) | 11.37s |
| **p95 Request Duration** | 12.5s | 5.74s | 29.99s (Timeouts started) |
| **Failure Rate** | 0% | 0% | 24.47% (Hit max limit) |

> [!TIP]
> **PgBouncer is extremely effective!** Just by adding it and scaling from 2 to 4 workers, our system was able to process nearly double the amount of requests in the same time frame without exhausting the database connections.

> [!WARNING]
> **Our max capacity is ~100 concurrent users.** At 100 VUs, the workers became saturated and couldn't process the Redis queue fast enough, causing 24% of the HTTP SSE streams to time out (hitting the 30-second k6 limit). 

## 🛠️ How to run large-scale tests yourself

Since you asked how to change this to test in large scale yourself, here is exactly how to do it!

### 1. Adjust the Load Profile
Open [load_test.js](file:///d:/personal/Production/support-agent/server/scripts/load_test.js) and modify the `options` block:
```javascript
export const options = {
  // Start with a ramp-up to find the breaking point gradually
  stages: [
    { duration: '30s', target: 50 },  // Ramp up to 50 users over 30s
    { duration: '1m', target: 100 },  // Ramp up to 100 users over 1 minute
    { duration: '30s', target: 200 }, // Push to 200 users to force failures
    { duration: '30s', target: 0 },   // Ramp down
  ],
};
```

### 2. Run the Test
Use the `k6` docker container to execute the script against the internal docker network:
```bash
docker run --rm -i -v ${PWD}/server/scripts:/scripts --network infra_default grafana/k6 run /scripts/load_test.js
```

### 3. Scale the Infrastructure
To handle larger scales (like 500+ users), you will need to increase the number of worker replicas and PgBouncer pool limits:
1. Open [docker-compose.yml](file:///d:/personal/Production/support-agent/infra/docker-compose.yml)
2. Change `worker > deploy > replicas: 4` to a higher number (e.g. `10` or `20`).
3. Ensure PgBouncer's `MAX_CLIENT_CONN` and `DEFAULT_POOL_SIZE` are high enough to support the workers.

> [!NOTE]
> Note that running 20+ LLM workers on a single machine during a load test will likely bottleneck your local CPU or your OpenAI/Anthropic API Rate Limits. True large-scale testing requires deploying to a cluster (like Kubernetes/EKS) and using a cloud-hosted Redis!
